The teaching model declares a sequentially consistent total order, atomic reads/writes and a stipulated atomic test-and-set equivalent, exact unbounded integer arithmetic, finite positive-increment job plans and no crashes or outside writers. Each actor has its own job index, instruction position and temporary value. It is a single-threaded Python enumerator of an abstract concurrent model, not a threaded application, language-memory implementation, processor test or host performance measurement. The source-level operation/atomic-step distinction is explicit.

Two single unit increments with read/add/write have twenty order-preserving schedules: eighteen lose an update, two serialize. With initial seven their final counters are eight and nine respectively. These counts are not probabilities without a scheduler distribution. The general complete-schedule count is (3Σq_i)!/∏(3q_i)!. An independent symbolic ancestry oracle follows the jobs represented by the surviving writes, separately from the numeric transition implementation. The plain two-job graph has 21 states after resetting completed local triples.

The locked acquire/read/add/write/release protocol has a complete inductive invariant: at most one holder; flag equals holder count; shared counter equals initial value plus completed jobs, plus the holder's increment only after its write. The holder's local value and positions are specified explicitly. Independent count-vector construction gives the entire reachable set and count ∏(q_i+1)+4Σ_i[q_i∏_(j≠i)(q_j+1)]. Two single jobs give twenty states and unique final counter two. Protected writes refine an abstract serial addition specification; a richer return/read/multi-field contract would require additional history reasoning. Split check/set and early release produce replayed wrong-final witnesses, with 72 and 34 states respectively for the two unit increments.

Failed acquisition attempts remain self-edges. Safety and existence of a completion path do not establish progress for all schedules. Finite-work termination is proved under weakly fair scheduling of continuously eligible actors, no failures and finite critical work. An unfair holder-unscheduled execution spins forever; with unbounded competing jobs even infinitely frequent scheduling can leave an actor forever unsuccessful at acquiring. The rank Σ(5c_i+p_i) advances on every non-spin step, giving exactly 5M+k steps with k failed attempts. Weak fairness does not give a finite bound on k. Fixed-work speedup is reused conditionally, with the explicit s=1/4,p=4 example 16/7 and asymptotic bound four.

The separate one-publisher/one-waiter monotone-predicate model declares an abstract atomic check/enrollment boundary, related to protected predicate checking and atomic release/enrollment/reacquisition in a condition wait. It does not implement a mutex or condition variable. The guarded graph has seven states with or without optional spurious wakes, no early finish or invalid terminal state and a completion path from each state. The defective gap model has eleven states and a lost-notification dead end without spurious wakes; the unchecked once model with spurious wakes has eight states and can finish while the predicate is false. Optional wake transitions do not guarantee eventual rescue. Richer competing-consumer behavior is explanatory preparation and assigned extension, not a claimed checked implementation.

