The progression derives positional encoding/range, modular addition, two’s-complement interpretation, carry versus signed overflow, Boolean full-adder equations and a carry invariant. It distinguishes combinational logic from stored state, simultaneous updates from sequential assignments, and a logical transition from physical timing. A separate exact 49-value floating model exposes spacing, a declared rounding rule and nonassociativity before the shared runtime example. Its nearest/lower-tie rule and omitted exceptional/subnormal categories are explicit; it is not an IEEE implementation.

The original machine has sixteen four-bit data locations, a separate immutable one-to-sixteen-byte program, accumulator, program counter and halted flag. Eight operations have 113 legal byte encodings. The complete semantics distinguish halt, reserved encoding, invalid taken target, next-fetch failure and exhausted budget. The sum program’s loop invariant proves the result n(n+1)/2 modulo 16 and termination in 8n+5 transitions for every four-bit input. Examples include n=4 yielding 10 in 37 transitions, n=6 yielding residue 5 in 53, and n=15 yielding residue 8 in 125. The modular contract is distinguished from an unbounded sum, and a wrong valid store address can violate the program without an immediate machine fault.

